I can't reach akamatsulab from here. The Discourse Graph MCP server has no graphs connected, and the Playwright test account only opens the dev graph. Even if you connected akamatsulab to the MCP server, its tools return pages as markdown and can't read the canvas data. The fastest way is two console snippets run in **your own browser**, with akamatsulab open.

## Where canvas data lives
A canvas's data is in its page's **block props**, under `roamjs-query-builder` (`useRoamStore.ts`):

| Key | Contents |
|---|---|
| `tldraw` | the canvas. Either a tldraw 2.x snapshot `{store, schema}`, or a **pre-2.x flat store with no schema** (the oldest format, upgraded through `LEGACY_SCHEMA` on load) |
| `legacyTldraw` | a backup kept when an old 1.x canvas was upgraded to 2.x. Its presence means the canvas has been through several tldraw versions. |
| `canvasSyncMode` | `"sync"` means it's a cloud canvas: its real data is in the cloud room, and the props copy is stale |

In older canvases, node shapes have `type` = the node type's uid, and arrows have `type` = the relation's uid. The scan below counts those separately.

## Step 1: find the richest old canvases
Open akamatsulab, open DevTools (Cmd+Opt+J), paste and run:
```js
(() => {
  const norm = (v) => Array.isArray(v) ? v.map(norm) : v && typeof v === "object"
    ? Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(v).map(([k, x]) => [k.replace(/^:+/, ""), norm(x)])) : v;
  const BUILTIN = new Set(["group","text","bookmark","draw","geo","note","line","frame","arrow","highlight","embed","image","video"]);
  const rows = window.roamAlphaAPI.data.q(
    "[:find ?title ?uid ?props :where [?p :node/title ?title] [?p :block/uid ?uid] [?p :block/props ?props]]");
  const out = [];
  for (const [title, uid, raw] of rows) {
    const q = norm(raw)?.["roamjs-query-builder"];
    if (!q?.tldraw && !q?.legacyTldraw) continue;
    const t = q.tldraw || {};
    const snap = !!(t.store && t.schema);
    const recs = Object.values(snap ? t.store : t);
    const shapes = recs.filter((r) => r?.typeName === "shape");
    const n = (k) => shapes.filter(k).length;
    const isNew = (s) => s.type === "discourse-node" || s.type === "discourse-relation" || BUILTIN.has(s.type);
    const isArrowLike = (s) => s.props && "start" in s.props && "end" in s.props;
    out.push({
      title, uid,
      mode: q.canvasSyncMode || "local",
      format: snap ? `2.x (schema v${t.schema.schemaVersion})` : "pre-2.x (no schema)",
      dgSchemaVersion: t.schema?.sequences?.["com.roam-research.discourse-graphs"] ?? "-",
      hasLegacyBackup: !!q.legacyTldraw,
      shapes: shapes.length,
      bindings: recs.filter((r) => r?.typeName === "binding").length,
      legacyNodeTyped: n((s) => !isNew(s) && !isArrowLike(s)),
      legacyRelationTyped: n((s) => !isNew(s) && isArrowLike(s)),
      discourseNode: n((s) => s.type === "discourse-node"),
      discourseRelation: n((s) => s.type === "discourse-relation"),
      plainArrows: n((s) => s.type === "arrow"),
    });
  }
  out.sort((a, b) => (b.hasLegacyBackup - a.hasLegacyBackup)
    || (b.legacyRelationTyped + b.legacyNodeTyped) - (a.legacyRelationTyped + a.legacyNodeTyped)
    || b.shapes - a.shapes);
  window.__dgCanvasScan = out;
  console.table(out.slice(0, 40));
})();
```
Good candidates have `hasLegacyBackup: true`, or `format: pre-2.x`, or a low `dgSchemaVersion` (≤4), **plus** many `legacyRelationTyped` arrows. Pick one or two `local` canvases. A `sync` canvas's props are stale, so its data has to come from the room instead.

## Step 2: export one canvas exactly as stored
Replace `PASTE_PAGE_UID` and run. It downloads `canvas-<uid>.json` with the full props (current data, legacy backup, schema) plus the titles of every node page the canvas references:
```js
(() => {
  const uid = "PASTE_PAGE_UID";
  const norm = (v) => Array.isArray(v) ? v.map(norm) : v && typeof v === "object"
    ? Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(v).map(([k, x]) => [k.replace(/^:+/, ""), norm(x)])) : v;
  const page = window.roamAlphaAPI.pull("[:node/title :block/props]", [":block/uid", uid]);
  const rjsqb = norm(page[":block/props"])["roamjs-query-builder"];
  const recs = Object.values(rjsqb.tldraw?.store ?? rjsqb.tldraw ?? {});
  const nodeUids = [...new Set(recs.filter((r) => r?.typeName === "shape" && r.props?.uid).map((r) => r.props.uid))];
  const nodePages = nodeUids.map((u) => {
    const p = window.roamAlphaAPI.pull("[:node/title :block/string]", [":block/uid", u]);
    return { uid: u, title: p?.[":node/title"] ?? null, string: p?.[":block/string"] ?? null };
  });
  const payload = { exportedAt: new Date().toISOString(), page: { uid, title: page[":node/title"] }, rjsqb, nodePages };
  const a = document.createElement("a");
  a.href = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2)], { type: "application/json" }));
  a.download = `canvas-${uid}.json`;
  a.click();
})();
```
Both snippets only read data; neither changes anything in akamatsulab. Save the file to `local/roam-playwright/eng1955/fixtures/` in the worktree, which is gitignored.

## Step 3: what I'd do with it (your call)
1. **An offline migration test (recommended first).** A script runs the exported snapshot through the full migration chain (pre-2.x upgrade, then v1–v6) with no Roam involved, and reports what each step changed: node types turned into `discourse-node`, uid-typed arrows into `discourse-relation`, and whether bindings stay intact. It's fast, repeatable, and keeps the real lab content off any shared graph.
2. **Import into the dev graph.** Write the props to a new `Canvas/…` page, and **create stub node pages with the same uids**. That second part matters: on load, the canvas deletes nodes whose page uid doesn't exist in the graph, along with their arrows, so without the stubs most of the canvas would vanish. Then open it with the `main` build and with the branch build.

One question before I build either: this is real akamatsulab research content (node titles and text). Is it OK to copy it into the shared dev graph as-is, or should option 2 replace titles with placeholders? Option 1 keeps it local either way.

