For a cloud canvas, the page's block props only hold a stale copy. The real data is in the cloud room, and the simplest way to get it is from the open canvas itself: once the canvas is loaded, its in-browser store mirrors the room. There are two ways.

## Option A: export from the open canvas (recommended)
The extension exposes the editor as `window.tldrawApps[title]` only in dev builds; production compiles that out, so in akamatsulab you have to find it through React instead.

1. In akamatsulab, open the cloud canvas page and **wait until it has fully loaded** (shapes visible, no "Loading Canvas"). Keep it in the main view, not the sidebar.
2. In DevTools (Cmd+Opt+J), run:
```js
(() => {
  const canvasEl = document.querySelector(".tl-canvas");
  if (!canvasEl) throw new Error("No open canvas found");
  const key = Object.keys(canvasEl).find((k) => k.startsWith("__reactFiber$"));
  let fiber = canvasEl[key], editor = null;
  while (fiber && !editor) {
    const p = fiber.memoizedProps;
    if (p?.value?.store && typeof p.value.getCurrentPageShapes === "function") editor = p.value;
    else if (p?.editor?.store) editor = p.editor;
    fiber = fiber.return;
  }
  if (!editor) throw new Error("Editor not found");
  const norm = (v) => Array.isArray(v) ? v.map(norm) : v && typeof v === "object"
    ? Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(v).map(([k, x]) => [k.replace(/^:+/, ""), norm(x)])) : v;
  const title = document.querySelector(".rm-title-display")?.innerText;
  const uid = window.roamAlphaAPI.q(`[:find ?u . :where [?p :node/title "${title}"] [?p :block/uid ?u]]`);
  const props = norm(window.roamAlphaAPI.pull("[:block/props]", [":block/uid", uid])?.[":block/props"] ?? {});
  const payload = {
    exportedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
    page: { uid, title },
    source: "cloud room, read from the live editor store",
    cloudSnapshot: editor.store.getStoreSnapshot(),   // { store, schema } as the client holds it
    rjsqb: props["roamjs-query-builder"],             // stale props copy, plus canvasSyncMode
  };
  const a = document.createElement("a");
  a.href = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2)], { type: "application/json" }));
  a.download = `cloud-canvas-${uid}.json`;
  a.click();
})();
```
It only reads data, and it downloads both the room contents (`cloudSnapshot`) and the stale props copy (`rjsqb`) so we can compare them.

**What this copy is and isn't:**
- **`cloudSnapshot.store`** is every record in the room, exactly as stored, since cloud data is never migrated. Old uid-typed arrows and old node types appear as they are.
- **`cloudSnapshot.schema`** is the *current extension's* schema, not the version the room data was written with. The room itself doesn't record our discourse-graphs version.
- **Opening the canvas is normal use, so it can still write to the room.** On load, the title sync removes nodes whose pages were deleted, and auto relations (if on) can add arrows. If you want the room's state before any of that, use option B.
- If more than one canvas is open (e.g. one in the sidebar), the snippet takes the first `.tl-canvas`. Close the others first.

## Option B: read the server's own copy from R2 (exact, no client involved)
The worker saves each room as JSON in the R2 bucket `tldraw-content`, under `rooms/<roomId>`. That file includes the room's own clock data and per-record metadata, and loading it in a client changes nothing.
- `roomId` = base64url of `{"graphName":"<graph>","pageUid":"<page uid>"}` (`getSyncRoomId` in `TldrawCanvasCloudflareSync.tsx`). In the Roam console, with the canvas page open:
  ```js
  btoa(JSON.stringify({ graphName: window.roamAlphaAPI.graph.name, pageUid: "<PAGE_UID>" }))
    .replace(/\+/g, "-").replace(/\//g, "_").replace(/=+$/, "")
  ```
- You then need access to the Discourse Graphs Cloudflare account: `wrangler r2 object get tldraw-content/rooms/<roomId> --file room.json` from `apps/tldraw-sync-worker`, or download it from the dashboard's R2 bucket browser. I don't have that access from here, and if you don't either, Michael likely does.

To find which canvases are cloud ones, the scan snippet from before lists them as `mode: "sync"` (`window.__dgCanvasScan.filter(c => c.mode === "sync")`).

Once you have a cloud export, send the path and I'll break it down like `Canvas/CIE`: which records are old-format, which are already migrated, and what v6 and the cloud legacy utils will do with them.

