content.text:
A canvas arrow doesn't store the uid of its Roam relation. You derive it from the arrow and then look up the matching block.

**How a reified relation is stored** (`utils/createReifiedBlock.ts`):
- It's a child block of the page **`roam/js/discourse-graph/relations`**. The block's text is its own uid.
- Its block props hold `discourse-graph: { sourceUid, destinationUid, hasSchema }`, plus optional `tentative` and `importedFrom`.
- `hasSchema` is the **relation type uid**. On the canvas that's `props.relationTypeId` (new arrows) or the arrow's `type` (old uid-typed arrows).
- `sourceUid` and `destinationUid` are the **Roam page uids of the two nodes**, which the canvas keeps in each node shape's `props.uid`.

So: arrow → its two bindings → the node shapes → their `props.uid` → query the relations page.

**Select the arrow on the canvas, then run this in the console:**
```js
(async () => {
  const el = document.querySelector(".tl-canvas");
  let f = el[Object.keys(el).find((k) => k.startsWith("__reactFiber$"))], editor = null;
  while (f && !editor) {
    const p = f.memoizedProps;
    editor = p?.value?.store && p.value.getCurrentPageShapes ? p.value : p?.editor?.store ? p.editor : null;
    f = f.return;
  }
  const arrow = editor.getSelectedShapes()[0];
  if (!arrow) return console.log("Select a relation arrow first.");
  const relTypeId = arrow.props.relationTypeId || arrow.type; // new vs legacy uid-typed arrow
  const bindings = editor.getBindingsFromShape(arrow, arrow.type);
  const end = (t) => editor.getShape(bindings.find((b) => b.props.terminal === t)?.toId);
  const [src, dst] = [end("start"), end("end")];
  const s = src?.props?.uid, d = dst?.props?.uid;
  console.log({ arrowId: arrow.id, shapeType: arrow.type, relTypeId, label: arrow.props.text,
                sourceNode: src?.props?.title, sourceUid: s, destNode: dst?.props?.title, destUid: d });
  if (!s || !d) return console.log("Arrow isn't bound to two discourse nodes.");

  const rows = await window.roamAlphaAPI.data.async.q(
    `[:find ?u ?data :where
      [?p :node/title "roam/js/discourse-graph/relations"] [?p :block/children ?c]
      [?c :block/uid ?u] [?c :block/props ?pr] [(get ?pr :discourse-graph) ?data]]`);
  const match = (a, b) => rows.filter(([, x]) =>
    x[":hasSchema"] === relTypeId && x[":sourceUid"] === a && x[":destinationUid"] === b);
  const fwd = match(s, d), rev = match(d, s); // complement-direction arrows are stored reversed
  if (!fwd.length && !rev.length) return console.log("NOT reified: no relation block for this arrow.");
  for (const [uid, x] of [...fwd, ...rev]) console.log(
    `REIFIED: block ${uid}  ${x[":sourceUid"] === s ? "same direction" : "stored reversed (complement)"}` +
    (x[":tentative"] === "true" ? "  [tentative, pending review]" : ""));
})();
```

How to read the result:
- **Prop key format:** the query returns prop keys with a leading `:` (`:hasSchema`). If your graph returns them without it, drop the colons in the `match` line.
- **"stored reversed":** if you drew the arrow from the relation's *destination* type to its *source* type (it shows the complement label, e.g. "Supported By"), it's saved with source and destination swapped. That's why the script checks both directions.
- **"NOT reified" doesn't always mean failure:**
  - **Stored relations off:** with "Reified relation triples" off (the dev graph's default before we turned it on), relations are written as linked text blocks instead, so no reified block is expected.
  - **Unknown relation type:** if the relation type doesn't exist in the graph, saving silently does nothing (`if (!relation) return;`).
  - **Drawn by an older build:** arrows from the fallback-only builds (0.22 and later, before this PR) were never saved to Roam, because their save step is a no-op.
- **To open the block:** `https://roamresearch.com/#/app/<graph>/page/<block uid>`, or search its uid in Roam.

